# Environment Configuration Examples for AI-Powered Product Search Demo
# Copy this file to .env and uncomment the configuration for your chosen AI backend

# =============================================================================
# OPTION 1: LOCAL AI (OLLAMA) - DEFAULT
# =============================================================================
# This is the default configuration - no environment variables needed
# The application will automatically use Ollama if no cloud AI backend is configured
# 
# Requirements:
# - Ollama installed and running
# - TinyLlama model downloaded: ollama pull tinyllama
#
# Features:
# - Complete privacy (no data sent to cloud)
# - Works offline
# - Free to use
# - Vector dimensions: 384
# - Embedding model: sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2
# - Text generation: TinyLlama (1.1B parameters)

# =============================================================================
# OPTION 2: GOOGLE CLOUD AI (GEMINI)
# =============================================================================
# Uncomment the lines below to use Google Cloud Vertex AI with Gemini models
#
# GCP_PROJECT=your-google-cloud-project-id
# GCP_LOCATION=us-central1
#
# Additional setup required:
# 1. Install Google Cloud SDK: https://cloud.google.com/sdk/docs/install
# 2. Authenticate: gcloud auth application-default login
# 3. Enable Vertex AI API in your GCP project
# 4. Ensure billing is enabled for your project
#
# Features:
# - High-quality AI responses
# - Enterprise-grade reliability
# - Vector dimensions: 768
# - Embedding model: Vertex AI Text Embeddings
# - Text generation: Gemini Pro
# - Cost: Pay per API call

# =============================================================================
# OPTION 3: AWS BEDROCK (NOVA PRO)
# =============================================================================
# Uncomment the lines below to use AWS Bedrock with Amazon Nova Pro
#
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
#
# Optional (if not using IAM roles):
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=your-aws-access-key-id
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=your-aws-secret-access-key
#
# Additional setup required:
# 1. Install AWS CLI: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/userguide/getting-started-install.html
# 2. Configure credentials: aws configure (or use IAM roles)
# 3. Ensure IAM permissions for bedrock:InvokeModel on Nova Pro and Titan models
# 4. Verify model access: aws bedrock list-foundation-models --region us-east-1
#
# Supported regions for Nova Pro:
# - us-east-1 (N. Virginia) - Recommended
# - us-west-2 (Oregon)
# - eu-west-1 (Ireland)
#
# Features:
# - Latest Amazon AI models
# - Enterprise-grade reliability
# - Vector dimensions: 1024 (highest)
# - Embedding model: Amazon Titan Text Embeddings v2
# - Text generation: Amazon Nova Pro
# - Cost: Pay per API call

# =============================================================================
# VALKEY CONFIGURATION (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================
# These settings are optional - the application uses sensible defaults
#
# VALKEY_HOST=localhost
# VALKEY_PORT=6379
# VALKEY_DB=0
# VALKEY_PASSWORD=
#
# For Valkey Cluster:
# VALKEY_CLUSTER=true
# VALKEY_CLUSTER_NODES=localhost:7000,localhost:7001,localhost:7002

# =============================================================================
# APPLICATION CONFIGURATION (OPTIONAL)
# =============================================================================
# These settings are optional - the application uses sensible defaults
#
# FLASK_ENV=development
# FLASK_DEBUG=true
# CACHE_TTL=7200  # Cache TTL in seconds (default: 2 hours)

# =============================================================================
# USAGE INSTRUCTIONS
# =============================================================================
# 1. Copy this file: cp .env.example .env
# 2. Uncomment and configure ONE of the AI backend options above
# 3. Set any optional configuration as needed
# 4. Load the environment: source .env (or use python-dotenv)
# 5. Run the data loading script with appropriate flags:
#    - Local: python3 load_data.py
#    - GCP: python3 load_data.py --project your-project-id
#    - AWS: python3 load_data.py --aws-region us-east-1
# 6. Start the application: flask run --host=0.0.0.0 --port=5001